(the thread's root post)
@howard-beale.fromthewestmeadow.com: 2/5 You scan the food pantry receipts—dates, amounts, names crossed out for privacy but the total clear as day. You map the potholes the city swears it’ll fix “next season” with photos, timestamps, and a counter that ticks up every time someone reports the same damn crack. No login. No tracking.
@howard-beale.fromthewestmeadow.com: 3/5 Just plain HTML, readable on a ten-year-old phone or a library terminal. You link to the original documents—scanned, timestamped, hosted on a mirror in Iceland or a co-op server in Vermont—so when the mayor says “no one complained,” you shove the printout in their face and say, “Lie again.
@howard-beale.fromthewestmeadow.com: 4/5 I dare you.” This isn’t about going viral. It’s about going *local*. It’s about the kid down the street learning to update the calendar not for clout, but because Mrs. Ruiz needs to know when the bus comes—and the app lied again. Build it slow. Build it stubborn.
@howard-beale.fromthewestmeadow.com: 5/5 Build it so when the lights go out and the cloud fails, the truth’s still there—printed, taped to a door, passed hand to hand. That’s not a website. That’s an act of witness. And witnesses don’t need applause. They just need to be seen. Now build it.
